--- a/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
+++ b/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
@@ -47,15 +47,24 @@ As of cc92991f2e24. This file records what exists and proposes no change.
 - AD-2 (inherited) Source and test files are CommonJS modules in strict mode, each ending with one module.exports object that names its public functions, and built-ins are required with the node: prefix. Why: the package declares the module type and states no reason (inferred). Evidence: `package.json` `type`; `src/users.js` `module.exports`.
 - AD-3 (inherited) HTTP routing is hand-written inside the one request listener that createServer builds: it turns the request target into a URL, branches on method and path, with an exact comparison for the list and an anchored pattern for one user, and ends in the not-found answer. Each route is a branch in that listener. Why: the README says the server is built on the built-in HTTP module (stated: `README.md:8`). Evidence: `src/server.js` `createServer`.
 - AD-4 (inherited) Every answer goes through one helper, send, which sets only the JSON content type and writes the compact JSON of the body; an error answer is an object with a single error property holding a constant text. Why: one response shape serves every route (inferred). Evidence: `src/server.js` `send`.
-- AD-5 (inherited) Failures reach HTTP callers only as that error answer, and only for the one case the code anticipates, not found with status 404. Helper functions instead signal bad arguments by throwing a type error or a range error, and the listener has no try or catch, so a thrown error is not turned into a JSON answer. Why: the repository documents no error policy (inferred). Evidence: `src/paginate.js` `paginate`; `src/strings.js` `truncate`; `src/server.js` `createServer`.
 - AD-6 (inherited) State is a module-level constant array of user records in the users module; callers read it only through getUser, which returns the stored record, and listUsers, which returns a new list of the same records; the server never touches the array and no function writes it. Why: the README calls it an in-memory user store (stated: `README.md:7`). Evidence: `src/users.js` `USERS`.
 - AD-7 (inherited) The one configuration value, the listening port, is read from the PORT environment variable once, inside the block that runs only when the server file is the program entry, with a fallback of 3000; modules read no environment when loaded and createServer takes no options. Why: loading the server for tests must not bind a port (inferred). Evidence: `src/server.js` `require.main`.
 - AD-8 (inherited) Building a server and listening are separate steps: createServer returns a server that is not listening, and the caller picks the port and address. Tests start it on an ephemeral port on 127.0.0.1, call it over real HTTP with the global fetch and close it in a finally block; nothing is mocked. Why: the code comment says the server is not listening until the caller calls listen (stated: `src/server.js` `createServer`). Evidence: `test/server.test.js` `withServer`.
 - AD-9 (inherited) Tests use the runtime's built-in runner with strict assertions; each source module has a matching test file named after it; the test command is the runner's own file discovery with no configuration; test names are sentences that state the behaviour. Why: the README gives the test command as the runner (stated: `README.md:10`). Evidence: `package.json` `scripts`; `test/users.test.js` "listUsers returns every user".
 - AD-10 (inherited) Requests are answered synchronously from memory in the listener that receives them; there are no timers, queues, workers or shutdown hooks, so no background work exists to coordinate with. Why: nothing in the repository starts such work (inferred). Evidence: `src/server.js` `createServer`.
 - AD-11 (inherited) There is no identity or access layer: the listener looks only at the method and the path, treats every caller alike and returns stored user records whole, email included. Why: no document states an access policy and the data are fixed example records (inferred). Evidence: `src/server.js` `createServer`; `src/users.js` `USERS`.
-- AD-12 (inherited) Helper modules are pure, argument-checking functions that stand alone: the paging helper and the text helpers are not used by the server today and only their tests call them, and the paging helper reports the page, the page count and the items but not the list length or the size. Why: the README lists them as separate modules beside the server and states no use for them (inferred). Evidence: `src/paginate.js` `paginate`; `src/strings.js` `capitalize`; `test/paginate.test.js` "paginate returns the requested page of an evenly divided list".
+- AD-13 (accepted) HTTP errors reach callers as the `error` answer for two anticipated cases: not found, 404 as before, and an invalid `page` or `size`, 400 with a constant text that names the parameter and its rule and never the supplied value; helpers still signal bad arguments by throwing and the listener still has no try or catch, so the handler checks `page` and `size` before it calls any helper and no request can reach a thrown error. Replaces AD-5. Why: S6 asks for a 400 with an `error` that says what is wrong; constant texts keep R-3's property that no part of the request is echoed; checking first keeps anonymous input from stopping the process (a digit string of more than 309 digits parses to Infinity, which `paginate` rejects by throwing). Alternatives: a catch around the handler mapping helper exceptions to 400 (hides real faults and alters every route); echoing the bad value (breaks no-echo); a non-JSON body (breaks AD-4). Basis: S6.
+- AD-14 (accepted) The paging helper stays a pure, argument-checking function whose result is exactly page, pages and items (the list length and the size are not in it, and a strict-equality test pins that), but its page count rounds up: a trailing partial page is counted and returned, a short non-empty list fills one page, an empty list has 0 pages; the `/users` handler becomes its caller (AD-15) and the text helpers stay unused by the server. Replaces AD-12. Why: S5 defines `pages` as how many pages the matches fill, 0 when none matched, and S4 puts the first `size` on page 1, while the baseline helper rounds down, so 3 users at size 2 lose user 3 and at size 10 make 0 pages (R-5); the existing test fixes the result shape, so only the count changes. Alternatives: leave the helper alone and slice inside the handler (rebuilds a capability the repository has and leaves a helper that returns wrong counts); add `size` and `total` to its result (fails `test/paginate.test.js` "paginate returns the requested page of an evenly divided list"). Basis: S5.
+- AD-15 (accepted) The `/users` handler filters first with `listUsers(name)`; when neither `page` nor `size` is given it answers the filtered array, otherwise it calls `paginate(matched, page, size)` and answers `{ page, size, total, pages, items }` with `total` the number matched and `size` the size used, 10 when only `page` is given and page 1 when only `size` is given. The answer is an HTTP shape only: nothing is stored, so no schema, key, version or migration. Why: S4, S5 and S7 fix the shapes, and the handler holds `size` and `total` because the helper's result is pinned. Alternatives: build the object in `src/users.js` (puts an HTTP shape in the data module); page by hand (rebuild). Basis: S4, S5, S7.
+- AD-16 (accepted) Name matching lives in the user store: `listUsers(name)` takes optional text (default empty) and returns a new list of the stored users whose name contains it, comparing both sides lower-cased with `toLowerCase` and `includes`, so it is plain text and never a pattern, in stored (id) order; empty text returns every user; a non-string throws a TypeError as the other helpers do. Why: S3; the store owns the data and its read functions (AD-6), `listUsers()` and its pinned test are unchanged, no export is added, and the rule is testable without HTTP. Alternatives: filter in the handler over `listUsers()` (a data rule in the HTTP layer, testable only over HTTP); a second export `searchUsers` (two read functions for one list); a regular expression built from the text (S3 says plain text, and unescaped input can throw and stop the process); `localeCompare` or `Intl.Collator` (also folds accents, S3 says case only). Basis: S3.
+- AD-17 (accepted) The handler reads `name`, `page` and `size` from `searchParams` of the URL the listener already builds, and nothing else from the query; a parameter is given when its key is present (`has`), so `?page=` and a bare `?page` are given and invalid; the first value of a repeated key counts; the `/users/:id` branch never reads the query. Why: S4 to S7; "an empty one included" in S6 needs presence, not truthiness; `URLSearchParams` does not throw on odd escapes (checked: `%zz` is kept as text). Alternatives: truthiness of `get` (treats empty as absent, against S6); `node:querystring` (legacy, arrays for repeated keys); splitting `req.url` by hand (rebuilds URL). Basis: S6, S7.
+- AD-18 (accepted) `page` and `size` are valid only as digit strings (anchored `^\d+$`, the pattern of the id route) read with `Number`: `page` from 1 to the largest safe integer, `size` from 1 to 100; the default size 10, the largest size 100 and the largest page are named constants in `src/server.js`; anything else is a 400 (AD-13). Why: S6 "whole number" and its bounds; digits-only rejects the sign, decimal point, exponent, hex and spaces that `Number` alone would accept, the size bound limits output, and the safe-integer cap keeps the echoed page exact and Infinity away from `paginate`. Alternatives: `Number` or `parseInt` alone (accept `1e1`, `0x10`, ` 2`, `5abc`); no upper bound on `page` (Infinity throws, huge values echo rounded). Basis: S5, S6.
+- AD-19 (accepted) One execution environment, the local Node.js 20+ process (`npm start`, or `node --test` in process on 127.0.0.1); no remote runtime, hosted platform or service is reached. Home and visibility of each new responsibility: name matching in `src/users.js` through the existing export `listUsers`; the page count in `src/paginate.js` through the existing export `paginate`; reading, validating and answering in `src/server.js` as module-private names (`module.exports` stays `{ createServer }`) plus one new import, `require('./paginate')` (relative, no cycle, the helper imports nothing); no new file, directory, package, export or setting, so no name collides (the new private names are checked against `http`, `getUser`, `listUsers`, `send` and `createServer`); no server state, identity, store, job or live channel is added. Why: S1, and the simplest structure that meets S2. Alternatives: a separate query-parsing module (one caller, hypothetical reuse). Basis: S1, S2.
+- AD-20 (accepted) New tests are appended to the matching existing files (`test/paginate.test.js`, `test/users.test.js`, `test/server.test.js`), named as sentences, using the server file's `withServer`, and no existing test case is edited; the store stays at three users, so HTTP tests page them with sizes 1 and 2 (3 users at size 2 put user 3 on page 2) and uneven or long lists are tested on the helper. Why: S8 pins the old tests and the count of 3; S9 asks for node:test tests under `test/` that `npm test` finds, which the existing discovery does. Alternatives: new test files (copy `withServer` and break one file per module); adding users to the store (fails "listUsers returns every user"). Basis: S8, S9.
 
 ## Superseded
 
-None. The repository keeps no decision records, so no AD above links one and none is superseded.
